<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tui-viti">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tui Viti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tui Viti, meaning 'king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>', is the title claimed in the mid-19th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</a>, the paramount chief of Bau. Accepted by some foreign consuls but disputed by rivals such as the Tongan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a>, the claim was realised in the Kingdom of Fiji under Cakobau in 1871. After the cession of Fiji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1874, Fijian chiefs applied the title to the British monarch.</p></div>
